1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Before you can build anything on Cloud, you have to understand the wire underneath it.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
The good news is that it's just REST over HTTP, which most people are familiar with if they've been developing.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
It's a style that you'll already know.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,000
This lesson gives you the ideas, the verbs, headers, and status codes you'll use on every call. Let's dig in.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Let's start with what REST actually means, because three ideas carry most of the weight.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,000
First, client-server.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Your code is the client, and Anthropx servers hold the model.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The two stay independent, so each side can change without breaking the other.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Second, stateless.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
This one matters a lot with Cloud.

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Every request has to carry all of its own context, because the server remembers nothing between calls.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
That's why you resend the whole conversation each time you want continuity.

13
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Third, a uniform interface.

14
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Resources live behind URLs, and you act on them with a small set of standard verbs.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Put those together, and you get one predictable pattern that covers the entire API,

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
which is exactly why REST is so easy to learn.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Now let's get concrete about what a Cloud call actually looks like on the wire.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
For the core of the API, almost everything you do is a single POST request to the messages endpoint.

19
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The path is version 1, then messages, and you send it over HTTPS, so the whole exchange is encrypted.

20
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Here's the part that surprises people coming from other APIs.

21
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
You don't put the model name in the URL.

22
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,000
The same endpoint serves every model.

23
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
You choose which one you want inside the request body instead.

24
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,000
So the shape is beautifully simple.

25
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,000
One verb, POST, one path, the messages endpoint.

26
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
And a JSON body that carries the model, your messages, and your settings.

27
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Get comfortable with that, because every feature you add later still rides on this same call.

28
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Two words come up constantly in REST, and they trip people up, so let's pin them down.

29
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,000
SAFE means a method doesn't change anything on the server.

30
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:14,000
A GET just reads, you can fire it 100 times, and the resource stays exactly as it was.

31
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,000
IDEMPOTENT is different.

32
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:21,000
It means calling the method once, or many times, leaves the server in the same final state.

33
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
A DELETE is IDEMPOTENT.

34
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Delete the same resource twice, and it is still just gone.

35
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Here's why this matters for you.

36
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
A POST is neither SAFE nor IDEMPOTENT, and the clawed messages call is a POST.

37
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Each call is a brand new generation.

38
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
So if a request times out and you blindly retry, you can get a second, separate completion.

39
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Retry logic has to account for that.

40
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Let's put the common HTTP verbs in one place, since you'll read them everywhere.

41
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
GET reads a resource and changes nothing.

42
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
POST creates something new or triggers an action that isn't meant to repeat.

43
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:04,000
PUT fully replaces a resource, while PATCH updates just part of it.

44
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,000
DELETE removes a resource, and running it again lands in the same place.

45
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Most REST APIs lean on this whole set.

46
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,000
The CLAWED API is simpler.

47
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
For generating a response, you almost always use POST,

48
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
because each completion is a fresh action, rather than a READ or a REPLACE.

49
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,000
So, while it helps to recognize all of these verbs when you read other people's code,

50
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
the one you'll type again and again against CLAWED is POST.

51
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Status codes come back on every response,

52
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:41,000
and the four families you'll actually see from this API are worth recognizing instantly.

53
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Anything in the 200s means success.

54
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
A 200 is the normal OK, and your body is ready to parse.

55
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:56,000
The 300s are redirects, and honestly, you'll rarely touch those directly with the CLAWED API.

56
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The 400s are the ones to watch, because they mean the problem is on your side.

57
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
BADAUTH, a malformed body, or a rate limit all live here.

58
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
The 500s mean the fault is on the server.

59
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Those aren't your bug, so the right move is to retry with BACKOFF rather than rewriting your request.

60
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Learn to read the first digit, and you'll know instantly whether to fix your call or simply try again.

61
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Before CLAWED ever sees your prompt, a couple of headers have to be right, or the call never lands.

62
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,000
The first is XAPIKEY.

63
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
That header carries your secret API key,

64
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
and it's how the server knows who you are and whether you're allowed in.

65
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
The second is ANTHROPICVERSION.

66
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
That one pins your request to a dated version of the API,

67
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
so behavior stays stable even as ANTHROPIC SHIPS changes.

68
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:52,000
It's required, though the SDKs set it for you automatically.

69
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,000
And of course, you send CONTENT TYPE as APPLICATION slash JSON for the body.

70
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Here's the thing to remember.

71
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
If you forget the key or the version header, you don't get a helpful model reply.

72
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:10,000
You get a 400 or a 401, and the request fails before any generation happens.

73
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Let's zoom in on the specific codes you'll actually hit while building.

74
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
First, the auth pair.

75
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:24,000
A 401 means you aren't authenticated, almost always a missing or wrong API key.

76
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
A 403 is different.

77
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,000
It means the key is valid, but you're not permitted to do that particular thing.

78
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Getting those two backwards wastes real debugging time, so keep them straight. Next, 429.

79
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
That's a rate limit.

80
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
You're sending faster than your tier allows,

81
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
so slow down and retry with BACKOFF instead of hammering the endpoint.

82
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
And a 400 means your request body itself is malformed.

83
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
That one won't fix itself on retry.

84
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
You have to correct the request before you send it again.

85
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Together, these three cover most of the errors you'll meet early on.

86
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Let's close the loop by connecting all of this to the SDKs,

87
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
because most of you won't handwrite these HTTP calls day to day.

88
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,000
The Anthropic SDKs in Python and TypeScript are convenience clients

89
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
that sit on top of the REST surface we just walked through.

90
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
When you call a method to create a message,

91
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
under the hood it's building exactly the POST request we described.

92
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
It sets your headers, attaches your API key,

93
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:35,000
handles retries, and parses the JSON response into typed objects

94
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
you can work with directly.

95
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
That's a real quality-of-life upgrade,

96
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
but here's the mental model to hold on to.

97
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
The SDK has no secret powers.

98
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Anything it can do maps to a REST call the API already supports.

99
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
So if the raw endpoint can't do something,

100
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:57,000
no SDK method can either. Let's recap.

101
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
The Cloud API is REST over HTTP,

102
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
which gives you one endpoint, a POST,

103
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
and a handful of headers and status codes.

104
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Keep in mind, XAPI key and Anthropic version are required,

105
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:15,000
and the first digit of a status code tells you where to look.

106
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,000
That covers REST fundamentals for the Cloud API.

107
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
Take a breath here, and I'll see you in the next one.

